WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:10.489
You read a status register and need to know whether bit five is set, without touching the others. That same masking trick wins interview problems.

00:00:11.689 --> 00:00:12.618
Pause and think.

00:00:13.018 --> 00:00:15.421
What does x AND x minus one do?

00:00:15.821 --> 00:00:23.135
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:30.430
This lesson is about bit tricks. You already use them in driver code; here is how interviews use them.

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:35.066
Think of an integer as a row of thirty two light switches.

00:00:35.516 --> 00:00:39.126
Each switch is a bit: one is on, zero is off.

00:00:39.576 --> 00:00:46.926
AND keeps a light on only if both rows have it on. OR turns it on if either does. XOR turns it on if exactly one does.

00:00:46.967 --> 00:00:58.472
New word: X O R. Exclusive OR: a bit operation that outputs 1 when the inputs differ. Picture it like this: A light that turns on only if exactly one of two switches is up.

00:01:00.117 --> 00:01:05.120
Shifting slides the whole row left or right, which is multiplying or dividing by two.

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:10.220
Let us look at the number twelve in binary: one one zero zero.

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:14.351
x and one tells you the lowest bit, so twelve is even.

00:01:14.801 --> 00:01:19.654
x and minus x isolates the lowest set bit. For twelve that is four.

00:01:20.104 --> 00:01:27.081
x and x minus one clears the lowest set bit. Twelve becomes eight. Repeat until zero to count the set bits.

00:01:28.431 --> 00:01:30.498
XOR has two magic rules.

00:01:30.948 --> 00:01:35.743
Any number XOR itself is zero. Any number XOR zero is itself.

00:01:36.193 --> 00:01:42.288
So in Single Number, XOR everything together, and the pairs cancel out, leaving the lonely number.

00:01:42.738 --> 00:01:50.795
RAID five storage uses the same rule: the parity block is the XOR of all data blocks, so any one lost block can be rebuilt.

00:01:50.833 --> 00:02:01.514
New word: RAID. Redundant Array of Independent Disks: combining disks for speed or safety. Picture it like this: Keeping copies of your notes in several notebooks.

00:02:04.064 --> 00:02:07.396
Bitmasks also store sets of small integers.

00:02:07.846 --> 00:02:12.630
Bit i set means item i is in the set. Union is OR, intersection is AND.

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:20.092
N Queens keeps three masks: taken columns and the two diagonal directions, so checking a square is one AND.

00:02:20.542 --> 00:02:26.254
The buddy allocator finds a block's partner with offset XOR size, flipping exactly one bit.

00:02:27.604 --> 00:02:29.578
Watch out for language gotchas.

00:02:30.028 --> 00:02:34.451
Java has no unsigned int, so use the triple right shift or a long.

00:02:34.901 --> 00:02:41.658
In C plus plus, shifting a signed negative number or shifting by thirty two or more is undefined behavior.

00:02:42.108 --> 00:02:47.205
C sharp has real unsigned types, uint and ulong, which make this simpler.

00:02:48.555 --> 00:02:53.606
Bits are cheap and fast. Practice Single Number, Counting Bits and Reverse Bits.

00:02:53.605 --> 00:02:54.859
Back to the question.

00:02:55.259 --> 00:02:57.662
What does x AND x minus one do?

00:02:58.062 --> 00:03:00.001
It clears the lowest set bit.

00:03:01.201 --> 00:03:02.072
Quick recap.

00:03:02.472 --> 00:03:04.399
An integer is a row of switches.

00:03:04.799 --> 00:03:07.249
Masks test, set and clear bits.

00:03:07.649 --> 00:03:09.425
XOR cancels pairs.
